<pre>
AI ≠ machine learning
AI ≠ generative AI
intelligent agent ≠ human-like intelligence
intelligent agent ≠ necessarily LLM-based
AI agent ≠ model
AI agent ≠ chatbot
AI agent ≠ necessarily fully autonomous
planning ≠ execution
planning ≠ chain-of-thought
tool use ≠ function calling only
tool use ≠ retrieval only
agent memory ≠ context window
agent memory ≠ vector database
agent memory ≠ RAG
agent memory ≠ human memory
multi-agent system ≠ distributed system
multi-agent system ≠ many model calls
autonomy ≠ absence of constraints
autonomy ≠ unsupervised learning
LLM ≠ chatbot
LLM ≠ agent
LLM ≠ foundation model by definition
LLM ≠ transformer by definition
</pre>

== Taxonomy correction ==

The wave preserves a three-layer distinction:

<pre>
Intelligent agent
  = established general computational abstraction

AI agent
  = contemporary applied AI-system term

Agentic AI
  = broader current architecture/research framing
</pre>

The first is historically stable.

The second and third remain terminology-sensitive and should not be presented as one universally standardized taxonomy.

== Planning correction ==

Classical automated planning is model-based reasoning over actions and goals.

Planning and acting are distinct:

<pre>
plan synthesis ≠ plan execution
</pre>

Real agents may require monitoring, replanning, continual deliberation, and execution feedback.

LLM-generated textual “plans” are one contemporary implementation pattern, not the definition of AI planning.

== Tool-use correction ==

Tool use can include:

<pre>
tool discovery / retrieval
→ selection
→ argument construction
→ invocation
→ execution feedback
→ result integration
→ multi-tool orchestration
</pre>

Function calling is one implementation mechanism.

A system can use tools without being a full autonomous agent.

== Agent-memory correction ==

Agent memory is an architecture-level persistence/recall capability.

Possible forms include:

* context-resident/token memory;
* external stores;
* retrieved factual memory;
* experiential/trajectory memory;
* persistent working state;
* parametric or latent memory mechanisms.

No one human-memory taxonomy is mandatory.

A vector database is one possible storage implementation, not the concept itself.

== Multi-agent correction ==

Multi-agent systems predate LLMs by decades.

The defining feature is multiple interacting computational agents.

A pipeline that simply calls several models in sequence is not necessarily a multi-agent system unless the components function as distinct agents with interaction/decision roles.

== Autonomy correction ==

Autonomy is graded.

An agent can be autonomous within:

* a fixed objective;
* a restricted action space;
* approval boundaries;
* budget/time limits;
* policy constraints;
* sandboxed environments.

Thus:

<pre>
autonomous ≠ unconstrained
</pre>

== LLM correction ==

An LLM is a language model, not an application architecture.

A chatbot can use one.

An agent can use one.

A retrieval system or tool orchestrator can wrap one.

The model itself remains distinct from those systems.

“Foundation model” emphasizes broad pretraining and downstream adaptability; “large language model” emphasizes large-scale language modeling.

== Source roles ==

* Russell & Norvig / classical AI texts — AI and intelligent-agent foundations.
* Wooldridge — intelligent agents and multi-agent systems.
* Ghallab, Nau & Traverso — planning and acting.
* NIST 2026 Agentic AI — current institutional agentic-AI terminology.
* Wang et al. (2024) and 2025–2026 surveys — LLM-based autonomous agents.
* Xu et al. (2025), COLING 2025 and 2026 tool surveys — tool learning/use/orchestration.
* Hu et al. (2025) and 2026 memory surveys — agent memory distinctions.
* Stanford HAI / CRFM — LLM and foundation-model distinction.
* standard NLP/deep-learning literature — language modeling.

